From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luke Craig <lacraig3@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 11:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] hw/core/loader: rom_reset logs the RAM it rewrites

rom_reset() puts each blob back into memory at reset in one of two ways:
through address_space_write_rom(), which marks the pages dirty, or, for a
blob with its own MemoryRegion (fw_cfg files such as etc/acpi/tables,
etc/table-loader), with a memcpy into the region's RAM, which does not.

Those regions are RAM blocks that migrate. During precopy, a guest reset
rewrites them without a dirty bit, so pages already sent are not sent
again: the destination keeps the bytes from before the reset (e.g. the
tables as acpi_build_update() had patched them) while the source holds the
blob's reset contents. The same holds for anything else that reads the
migration bitmap; an in-memory snapshot that copies back only dirty pages
left x86's etc/acpi/tables with the wrong bytes after a reset.

Mark the rewritten range dirty, as the other path does and as
acpi_ram_update() does for the same regions. The memcpy path runs only at
reset, so this costs one bitmap update per blob per reset.
---
 hw/core/loader.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/core/loader.c b/hw/core/loader.c
index 5cbfba0a86d24569923c863d6ecda125ce43bceb..8da69dcfc7e325c879748de7c05270c05288e801 100644
--- a/hw/core/loader.c
+++ b/hw/core/loader.c
@@ -1270,6 +1270,14 @@ static void rom_reset(void *unused)
             void *host = memory_region_get_ram_ptr(rom->mr);
             memcpy(host, rom->data, rom->datasize);
             memset(host + rom->datasize, 0, rom->romsize - rom->datasize);
+            /*
+             * Log the rewrite as address_space_write_rom() does on the other
+             * path: dirty-memory clients (migration, in-memory snapshots that
+             * copy back only dirty pages) must see that this RAM changed.
+             */
+            memory_region_set_dirty(rom->mr, 0,
+                                    MIN(rom->romsize,
+                                        memory_region_size(rom->mr)));
         } else {
             address_space_write_rom(rom->as, rom->addr, MEMTXATTRS_UNSPECIFIED,
                                     rom->data, rom->datasize);
